{
  "title": "Lateral Ulnar Collateral Ligament Injury (PLRI)",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/shoulder-and-elbow/3129/lateral-ulnar-collateral-ligament-injury-plri",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Apr 28 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Lateral Ulnar Collateral Ligament Injury is a ligamentous elbow injury usually associated with a traumatic elbow dislocation, and characterized by posterolateral subluxation or dislocation of the radiocapitellar and ulnohumeral joints.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made with plain radiographs of the elbow which may show an isolated elbow dislocation or an elbow dislocation with a radial head and coronoid tip fracture.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment may be nonoperative or operative depending on presence of concomitant elbow fractures, as well as elbow stability following reduction.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**traumatic**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "most often discussed as a result of **elbow dislocation**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "combination of forearm **supination, axial loading, valgus** (posterolateral) stress, and elbow extension causes progressive failure of the lateral collateral ligament complex and anterior capsule, resulting posterolateral subluxation of the radial head and external rotation of the semilunar notch away from trochlea",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3129/images/mechanism.jpg",
                          "caption": ""
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "Lateral collateral ligament complex most commonly fails at humeral origin (lateral epicondyle)",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "-1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "common extensor origin can also be avulsed",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "radioulnar articulation remains intact",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**iatrogenic injury**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "from arthroscopic or open procedures of the lateral elbow that go posterior to equator of radial head (e.g. debridement of lateral epicondylitis)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "arthroscopic debridement should be kept anterior to equator of the radial head",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**chronic attenuation**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "secondary to chronic cubitus **varus malunion**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "abnormal lateral thrust stretches out the LUCL with time",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "abnormal triceps vector further stretches LUCL",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**elbow dislocations**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Lateral collateral ligament complex consists of 4 components",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3129/images/lucl%20anatomy%20image_moved.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**accessory lateral collateral ligament**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**annular ligament**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**lateral radial collateral ligament** (LCL)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**lateral ulnar collateral ligament** (LUCL)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "LUCL is the primary stabilizer to varus & ER stress",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "origin",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "lateral humeral epicondyle",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "insertion",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "the tubercle of the supinator crest of the ulna",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**pain** is the primary symptom",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**mechanical symptoms** (clicking, catching, etc.)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "often with elbow extension and when pushing off from arm of chair",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "inspection and palpation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**tenderness over LUCL**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "motion and stability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**varus instability**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "provocative tests",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**lateral pivot-shift test**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "patient lies supine with affected arm overhead; forearm is supinated and valgus stress is applied while bringing the elbow from full extension to 40 degrees of flexion",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "with increased flexion, triceps tension reduces the radial head",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "often more reliable on anesthetized patient",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "posterior drawer test",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "patient lies supine with affected arm overhead; forearm is supinated and the examiner's index finger is placed under the radial head and the thumb over it.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "application of a posterior force will cause posterior subluxation of the radial head",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "apprehension test",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "patient lies supine with affected arm extended overhead; forearm is supinated and valgus stress is applied while flexing the elbow",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**chair rise test**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**table-top** relocation test",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "floor push-up test",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "patient cannot do push-ups with forearm supinated",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recommended views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "AP and lateral views of elbow",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "important to rule out associated fractures and confirm concentric reduction in setting of acute dislocation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "standard radiographs are often of little value in evaluating PLRI",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "fluoroscopic imaging during provocative testing (e.g. pivot-shift) may demonstrate radial head subluxation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "may not be helpful in the setting of recurrent instability and LUCL attenuation as visualizing ligament difficult due to oblique course",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "can identify acute avulsion of LUCL in acute instability",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3129/images/lucl.jpg",
                      "caption": "Coronal T2 MRI of elbow with LCL rupture"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "sensitivity and specificity",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "LUCL pathology identifed in 50% of patients",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Differential",
      "table": null,
